To learn if a partition is in either positive or negative half-
spaces (i.e., Θ⊆h+orΘ⊆h−), it suffices to check the
relationship of a utility vector u∈Θand hyper-plane h. If
u∈h+(resp. u∈h−), then Θ⊆h+(resp. Θ⊆h−).
For the batch size γ, a small one might necessitate more
iterations (i.e., repeating more times the exploration and inter-
action phases), prolonging the whole process, while a large one
could lead to a large number of partitions, causing a high com-
putational cost. We will discuss the setting of γin Section VII.
Challenge 2. In the exploration phase, utility range Ris
divided into partitions by the hyper-planes in the current batch.
Then, in the interaction phase, we expect to delete partitions
until there is only one left with a minimal number of interac-
tive rounds. Let us formalize the interaction phase by a binary
tree, called decision tree orD-Tree for short. In the D-Tree,
